<TopicPage slug="subjects/religious-studies/advanced/scripture-and-interpretation">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This lesson asks the question underneath the whole intermediate tier. It assumes the traditions you have
    studied there — <em>Christianity</em>, <em>Islam</em>, <em>Judaism</em>, and the methodological ground of
    <em>Studying Religion Academically</em> — because every doctrine you met there arrives as a reading of a text.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>tell the three canon stories — Tanakh, New Testament, Qurʾan — and explain why canon is a community's first interpretation;</li>
    <li>trace doctrine back to interpretation: the creedal phrasings, the six articles and five roots, the covenants;</li>
    <li>run the medieval fourfold sense on a text and say what each level delivers;</li>
    <li>compare where traditions locate interpretive authority — magisterium, usul al-fiqh, responsa, marjaʿiyyah, text within community;</li>
    <li>explain why fundamentalism is itself a modern interpretive style, and why translation is hermeneutics.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The intermediate lessons set out the beliefs of Christianity, Islam and Judaism — God, Jesus and the
    Spirit; the articles of faith and the Five Pillars; G-d's nature and the covenants. This lesson asks the
    question underneath all of them: <strong>how do communities get doctrine out of text?</strong> Scripture is
    never text alone; it is text plus community plus interpretive method, and the method determines what the
    tradition can become. Every belief you have studied is an act of interpretation that was selected,
    argued over and institutionalised — and each tradition has developed a distinctive, sophisticated
    hermeneutic for doing it.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="canon, interpretive styles, fundamentalism as modernity, translation" widget="equationStep" config={scriptCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four cards are one argument in stages — communities select texts (canon),
    build methods for reading them (the interpretive styles), react when modern criticism arrives (fundamentalism
    as a modern style), and decide what may cross languages (translation politics). Watch the through-line:
    nowhere is the text left to speak for itself — each card relocates authority, into the list of books, the
    method, the reaction, or the language.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> scripture is text plus community plus interpretive method, and the method decides
    what the tradition can become. We begin where every tradition began: which books count?
  </p>

  <h2>Canon: how books became THE Book</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The Hebrew Bible</strong> settled as the Tanakh — Torah (teaching), Neviʾim (prophets), Ketuvim
    (writings) — across the Second Temple period and after, with rabbinic debate about individual books
    (Ecclesiastes "defiles the hands" — touches — but was included) recorded in the Mishnah. <strong>Early
    Christianity</strong> inherited the Greek Jewish Scriptures (the Septuagint, with books later called
    apocrypha or deuterocanon) and added its own writings; the twenty-seven-book New Testament list appears in
    Athanasius's Easter letter of 367 and was affirmed by councils at Hippo (393) and Carthage (397) — the
    boundary drawn by argument about apostolicity and use in worship. <strong>The Qurʾan</strong> took a
    different route to closure: revealed orally over some twenty-two years, compiled under the caliph Uthman
    (c. 650) into a standard text; its authority rests on unbroken chains of recitation as much as on
    manuscripts. The pattern matters because canon is the community's first interpretation: <em>which</em>
    texts count decides in advance what the tradition can hear God saying.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="scrub the canon's closing dates" widget="timeline" config={scrCanonTimeline} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the Bible and the Qurʾan reached their present form the same way — either a
  fixed list from the start, or a committee's arbitrary pick." Both pictures miss the documented processes. The
  Hebrew canon settled across the Second Temple period and after, with individual books debated in the Mishnah;
  the twenty-seven-book New Testament list appears in Athanasius's Easter letter of 367 and was affirmed at Hippo
  and Carthage by argument about apostolicity and use in worship; the Qurʾan was standardised under Uthman within
  roughly two decades, its authority resting on unbroken chains of recitation. The correct picture: canon is the
  community's first interpretation — <em>which</em> texts count decides in advance what the tradition can hear —
  and closure's route and speed differ tellingly.</p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="find the faulty line — a canon briefing" widget="spot-error" config={scrCanonSpot} />

  <h2>What the texts are read to say: the beliefs they carry</h2>
  <p>
    The syllabus's beliefs are, concretely, interpretations. <strong>Christian</strong> doctrines of one God in
    trinity, of Jesus as fully divine and human, and of the Holy Spirit as Lord and giver of life were fixed by
    reading Scripture against early controversies — "begotten, not made" (Nicaea, 325) is a phrase chosen
    because Arius's alternative was ruled out by the texts as read at the time. <strong>Islamic</strong> belief
    is organised textually too: the <strong>six articles of faith</strong> (God, angels, revealed books,
    messengers, the Last Day, divine decree) and, in Twelver Shiʿism, the <strong>five roots of Usul ad-Din</strong>
    (divine unity, divine justice, prophethood, <em>imamah</em> — the God-appointed leadership of the community
    through Ali and the Imams — and the Day of Resurrection) are readings of the Qurʾan; the
    <strong>Five Pillars</strong> (shahadah, salah, zakah, sawm, hajj) rest on the Sunnah, the recorded practice
    of <strong>Prophet Muhammad</strong> — whose life, collected in hadith with graded chains of transmission
    (isnad), functions as interpretation made flesh. The <strong>Sunni–Shiʿa divide itself</strong> began as a
    hermeneutic of authority: after the Prophet's death in 632, should the community follow consensus around
    Abu Bakr, or the divinely designated line through Ali? Each side then developed the texts to support its
    answer. <strong>Judaism</strong> reads G-d as one, creator, transcendent yet immanent (the <em>Shekhinah</em>,
    the indwelling presence), lawgiver and redeemer — and structures the relationship through
    <strong>covenants</strong>: with Abraham (land, descendants, blessing), sealed at Sinai with the whole
    people (Exodus 19–24, the covenant code), and the Davidic promise. Doctrine, in all three cases, is the
    settled residue of interpretive practice.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="file each belief-structure under its tradition" widget="sort-buckets" config={scrBeliefSort} />

  <h2>Interpretation, tradition by tradition</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Judaism treats interpretation as sanctified argument.</strong> The rabbis held that Moses received
    both a written Torah and an <em>oral</em> Torah at Sinai — the interpretive tradition is thus revelation,
    not mere commentary. <strong>Midrash</strong> exploits the text's gaps, juxtapositions and odd spellings to
    generate law and story; the <strong>Talmud</strong> preserves the arguments themselves (the dissenting view
    is recorded, not erased — "these and these are the words of the living G-d"). The classic fourfold scheme,
    <strong>PaRDeS</strong>, reads on four levels: <em>peshat</em> (plain sense), <em>remez</em> (allusive
    hint), <em>derash</em> (homiletical inquiry), <em>sod</em> (mystical secret). Rashi's eleventh-century
    commentary became the standard because it keeps all levels in view. Modernity split the tradition: for
    Orthodoxy the Torah is divine in origin and binding; Reform Judaism reads the Torah as a human document
    with divine inspiration, under ongoing reinterpretation — the interpretive method <em>is</em> the
    denomination.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="pair each PaRDeS level with what it reads for" widget="match" config={scrPardesMatch} />
  <p>
    <strong>Christianity's history is a sequence of hermeneutics.</strong> Origen and Augustine saved Greek
    philosophy for the faith through allegory; the medievals formalised the <strong>fourfold sense</strong> —
    literal, allegorical (what to believe), moral (what to do), anagogical (what to hope) — summed in the
    couplet "the letter teaches events, allegory what you shall believe..." The Reformation's
    <em>sola scriptura</em> transferred authority to the plain text in the vernacular (Luther's Bible, the King
    James of 1611) — and the plain text then required philology, which matured into <strong>historical
    criticism</strong>: source criticism (Pentateuch sources; the Q source behind Matthew and Luke), form
    criticism, redaction criticism — reading the Bible as documents with dates, sources and agendas.
    <strong>Fundamentalism</strong> is the modern counter-move, named from <em>The Fundamentals</em>
    (1910–15): asserting inerrancy and "literal" reading against critical scholarship. Scholars note the
    irony that the literalism is itself a modern style, born in reaction — a stubborn text did not exist
    before there was a method to resist. In Islam, <strong>tafsir</strong> is the layered commentary tradition
    (al-Tabari, al-Razi), disciplined by <em>asbab al-nuzul</em> (the occasions of revelation) and principles
    like abrogation; <strong>ijtihad</strong> is independent reasoning to derive law, its classical
    "closing of the gates" now doubted by historians — the gates of scholarly debate demonstrably never fully
    shut. Shiʿi interpretation adds <em>ta'wil</em> — esoteric reading authorised by the Imams — which is
    precisely why Imamah, the doctrine of interpreting authority, sits among the roots of the religion.
    And translation is itself hermeneutics: Islam classically treats the Arabic Qurʾan as the revelation
    itself, so printed translations are titled "interpretations of the meanings" — a compromise that tells
    you exactly where the community located the sacred.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="true or false — the modern methods" widget="judge" config={scrHermJudge} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "interpretation means anything goes." In practice every tradition has
  stringent boundary controls — recognised interpreters, accepted methods, heresy trials, chains of
  transmission. The question is never whether to interpret (you must, to read at all) but <em>whose</em>
  interpretation counts, and by what rules.</p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="fill the blank — the boundary controls" widget="cloze" config={scrBoundaryCloze} />

  <h2>One verse, four senses: the medieval method, worked</h2>
  <p>
    The fourfold sense is best learned by running it. Take Psalm 23 — "The Lord is my shepherd." The
    <strong>literal</strong> sense: a psalm of trust, voicing the experience of divine care in the image of
    a shepherd's vigilance — the historical poet's meaning, which the other levels must respect, never
    replace. The <strong>allegorical</strong> sense (what to believe): the shepherd is read as Christ,
    the "good shepherd" of John 10 — the Old Testament image received as pointing forward. The
    <strong>moral</strong> sense (what to do): the hearer is the sheep — the psalm becomes guidance for
    the soul that trusts through dark valleys rather than fleeing them. The <strong>anagogical</strong>
    sense (what to hope): the journey through the valley issues in dwelling "in the house of the Lord
    forever" — final union with God. Notice what the method achieves: it lets a single ancient poem
    address doctrine, ethics and destiny at once, in a controlled, teachable way — and notice its modern
    vulnerability, which the Reformation pressed: who authorises the allegory? Once the plain sense in
    the vernacular became available, the fourfold structure required an interpreter with authority —
    which is exactly the argument the tradition has been having ever since.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="walk the Reformation's challenge" widget="branch" config={scrAuthorityBranch} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the fourfold method shows interpretation at its most controlled — one poem, four
    levels, all teachable. Its vulnerability is the question the Reformation pressed: who authorises the reading?
  </p>

  <h2>Authority in practice: how each tradition licenses its readers</h2>
  <p>
    The boundary controls, concretely. <strong>Sunni Islam</strong> codified its method as
    <em>usul al-fiqh</em> — the "roots of law": the Qurʾan, the Sunnah, then <em>ijmaʿ</em> (scholarly
    consensus) and <em>qiyas</em> (analogical reasoning from established cases — extending the prohibition
    of intoxicants to novel drugs, for instance). The four recognised schools of law (Hanafi, Maliki,
    Shafiʿi, Hanbali) are rival method-sets within one frame, and taqlid — following a qualified scholar —
    is precisely the licensing mechanism. <strong>Twelver Shiʿism</strong> relocates authority in the
    Imams' teaching and, after the twelfth Imam's occultation, in the marjaʿiyyah — senior jurists believers
    emulate; which is why <em>imamah</em> is a root of the religion, not a footnote. <strong>Judaism</strong>
    authorises through <strong>responsa</strong> — centuries of written rulings answering real cases, each
    citing its sources, forming a living chain (the <em>shulchan aruch</em> codified; modern movements
    extend the chain to their own committees). <strong>Catholic Christianity</strong> adds the magisterium —
    the teaching office that alone defines doctrine — while Protestantism locates authority in Scripture
    read within the community, with confessing documents (creeds, confessions) as subordinate standards.
    The comparative point is the one the syllabus wants: <em>every</em> tradition has solved the same
    problem — who may say what the text means — and the solutions differ in where they locate the authority
    (office, consensus, lineage, or text alone).
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "sola scriptura means everyone reads the plain text and no interpretation is
  needed." The plain text is the problem: Luther's vernacular Bible immediately required philology, which
  matured into the historical-critical methods, and Reformation communities still wrote creeds and confessions
  as subordinate standards. It seems reasonable to picture the text as self-interpreting; the correct picture is
  the lesson's comparative point — every tradition must solve who may say what the text means, and
  Protestantism's solution (Scripture read within community, under confessing documents) locates that authority
  differently, not nowhere.</p>

  <h2>Canon, worked: what gets in and why</h2>
  <p>
    The canon debates reward one concrete case study. The Book of Enoch — an ancient Jewish apocalypse
    quoted as scripture in the Epistle of Jude — made it into the Ethiopic Orthodox canon, but not the
    Hebrew, Catholic, or Protestant Bibles. Its fortunes trace every criterion at work: apostolic
    citation (arguably for), authorship claims (against — pseudepigraphic), use in worship (against in
    the west), and community tradition (for, in Ethiopia — where the canon was never closed by the same
    councils). The case shows that "the canon" is not one list but several, each the residue of its
    community's arguments; the same logic explains the deuterocanonical books (Tobit, Maccabees and
    companions — in Catholic and Orthodox Bibles since they followed the Greek Septuagint, excluded by
    Protestants who followed the Hebrew canon at the Reformation). A parallel Islamic contrast sharpens
    the point: the Qurʾan's canon was closed within about two decades of the Prophet's death by a single
    authority decision (Uthman's standardisation), so Islam never had the centuries-long canonical
    argument Christianity had — and the interpretive energy went instead into <em>reading</em> the fixed
    text. Canon formation's speed, in other words, shapes where a tradition's intellectual labour
    happens for the next thousand years.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Run the fourfold sense on "The Lord is my shepherd" from memory: what does each of the four levels
    deliver, and which level must the others respect?</li>
    <li>The Book of Enoch is scripture in Ethiopia but not in the Hebrew, Catholic or Protestant Bibles. Use the
    canon criteria to explain how one book can be both in and out.</li>
    <li>Sunni <em>usul al-fiqh</em> and the Catholic magisterium both control interpretation. What differs in
    where each locates authority — and what stays the same?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Literal: the psalm of trust, the historical poet's meaning — the level the
    others must respect, never replace. Allegorical: what to believe (the shepherd read as Christ, the good
    shepherd of John 10). Moral: what to do (the hearer as the sheep, trusting through dark valleys).
    Anagogical: what to hope (dwelling in the house of the Lord forever). (2) The criteria conflict by community:
    apostolic citation argues for Enoch (Jude quotes it as scripture), pseudepigraphic authorship argues against,
    and use in worship plus community tradition resolve differently in Ethiopia, where the canon was never closed
    by the same councils. (3) The Sunni method rests on sources plus scholarly consensus and analogy, with taqlid
    licensing qualified interpreters; the magisterium locates authority in a teaching office that alone defines
    doctrine. The constant: neither leaves the text to private reading — whose interpretation counts is the
    shared question.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Scripture is text plus community plus interpretive method — and the method determines what the tradition can become.</li>
    <li>Canon is the community's first interpretation; how fast it closed shaped where each tradition's intellectual labour went.</li>
    <li>Doctrine is settled interpretation: the creedal phrasings, the six articles and five roots, the covenants.</li>
    <li>The fourfold sense (literal, allegorical, moral, anagogical) lets one poem address belief, conduct and destiny in a controlled way.</li>
    <li>Fundamentalism is a modern interpretive style, born against historical criticism — not the tradition's default state.</li>
    <li>Authority is the real variable: office, consensus, lineage, responsa chains, or text read within community.</li>
    <li>Translation is hermeneutics — the Qurʾan's "interpretations of the meanings" says exactly where the community located the sacred.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    <em>Myth, Ritual &amp; Symbol</em> is the sibling advanced lesson: where this chapter follows the authority of
    texts, it follows the power of enacted and symbolic forms — the two channels through which traditions
    transmit. The intermediate <em>Studying Religion Academically</em> rereads differently now: its method
    questions are, concretely, questions about who may interpret.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Textual criticism operates underneath all of this.</strong> The Hebrew Bible's manuscripts (the
    Leningrad Codex of 1008; the Dead Sea Scrolls, found from 1947, a thousand years older) show a
    remarkably stable text with genuine variants — Jeremiah, for instance, runs substantially shorter in the
    Greek Septuagint. The New Testament's some 5,800 Greek manuscripts (including the fourth-century Codex
    Sinaiticus) differ in hundreds of thousands of small ways, almost all trivial, a few doctrinally
    consequential (the longer ending of Mark; the Johannine Comma) — reconstructed by the very historical
    methods the fundamentalist controversy contested. Qurʾanic codicology is younger but active: the
    Ṣanʿāʾ palimpsest discovered in 1972 preserves an undertext with variant readings, fuelling careful
    scholarly debate about the earliest text's transmission. <strong>Hermeneutics as a general theory</strong>
    (Schleiermacher, Gadamer) claims all understanding is interpretive — prejudices are not obstacles but the
    enabling conditions of understanding, and a text's meaning is an event in the meeting of horizons. That is
    why the subject is everyone's: constitutional originalism versus the living document, strict construction
    versus judicial precedent, even disputed readings of data and rules are scripture-interpretation debates
    in secular dress.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="call the manuscript gap" widget="estimate" config={scrScrollsEst} />
  <p>
    Open questions: can a tradition's authoritative interpretation be revised without unravelling authority
    itself (the live question behind every schism)? Do digital humanities — searchable corpora of hadith and
    manuscripts — democratise interpretation or fragment it into rival micro-traditions? And is there such a
    thing as reading a text "as it is," or only ever reading it within a horizon — the oldest question in
    hermeneutics, now posed with the largest stakes.
  </p>

